Felectron Learning: A Categorical Rearchitecture of Machine Learning for Extreme Data Efficiency

A Proposal for a Category-Theoretic Foundation of Learning

Abstract

Modern machine learning has achieved remarkable capabilities by combining large neural architectures with enormous quantities of training data and computational resources. This paper proposes a different direction: Felectron Learning, a theoretical framework that rearchitects machine learning around category theory, compositional representations, and a hypothetical information-bearing entity called the Felectron.

The central objective of Felectron Learning is straightforward:

Greatly reduce the quantity of training data required for a machine-learning system to acquire useful generalizable knowledge.

Rather than treating every training example as an independent source of information, Felectron Learning proposes that learning systems should discover the relationships, transformations, equivalences, and compositional structures shared across examples. Category theory provides a natural mathematical language for this objective because it emphasizes objects, morphisms, composition, identity, and structure-preserving transformations.

In this framework, a model does not primarily learn a massive collection of individual examples. It attempts to construct a categorical theory of the world represented by its data.

The Felectron becomes the fundamental theoretical unit of learnable information, while the Felectron Field represents the evolving organization of that information. Learning is consequently interpreted as the construction, transformation, and compression of categorical structure.

This paper presents the conceptual architecture, mathematical foundations, learning objective, proposed training mechanism, and research agenda of Felectron Learning.

