1. Introduction

The dominant paradigm of modern machine learning can be summarized approximately as:

\text{More Data} + \text{More Parameters} + \text{More Compute}
\rightarrow
\text{Better Models}

This paradigm has been extraordinarily successful.

However, it raises a fundamental question:

Must intelligence require enormous quantities of examples?

Humans frequently learn concepts from remarkably few observations.

A child can encounter a new animal once or twice and subsequently recognize related examples. A mathematician can learn an entire family of objects from a small number of axioms. A scientist can infer an underlying law from a limited set of observations.

This suggests that intelligent learning may depend less on memorizing observations and more on discovering structure.

Felectron Learning proposes that machine learning should therefore be rearchitected around a different principle:

\boxed{
\text{Learn Structure Before Memorizing Instances}
}

The goal is not merely to make existing machine-learning algorithms more efficient.

The goal is to construct a different theoretical foundation for learning.

