13. Structural Generalization

Suppose a model sees:

A\rightarrow B

and:

B\rightarrow C

A conventional system may need additional examples showing:

A\rightarrow C

A categorical system can potentially derive:

A\rightarrow C

through:

g\circ f

This is the fundamental promise of categorical learning:

Generalization can arise from composition rather than additional memorization.

If the world contains reusable structures, a learner capable of discovering those structures should require fewer observations.

