16. Neural Networks as Approximate Functors

Rather than discarding neural networks, Felectron Learning proposes reinterpreting them.

A neural network can be viewed as an approximate mapping:

F:\mathcal C_{\text{world}}\rightarrow\mathcal C_{\text{representation}}

The problem becomes determining how well:

F(g\circ f)
\approx
F(g)\circ F(f)

holds.

This produces a new training objective:

\mathcal L_{\text{total}}
=
\mathcal L_{\text{task}}
+
\lambda
\mathcal L_{\text{functor}}

where:

\mathcal L_{\text{functor}}
=
d\left(
F(g\circ f),
F(g)\circ F(f)
\right)

penalizes violations of compositional structure.

